<http://dbpedia.org/resource/South_African_War_Memorial_(Toronto)> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The **South African War Memorial** is a memorial located at [University Avenue](:University_Avenue_%28Toronto%29) and [Queen Street West](:Queen_Street_West) in [Toronto](:Toronto), Ontario, Canada.Commissioned in 1910, largely as the result of the efforts of [James Mason](:James_Mason_%28Canadian_politician%29), and designed by [Walter Seymour Allward](:Walter_Seymour_Allward) to commemorate [Canada](:Canada)'s participation in the [Boer War](:Second_Boer_War), it consists of three bronze figures at the base of a granite column. Another bronze figure is found at the top of the memorial. It was restored in 2001.The [Ontario Heritage Foundation](:Ontario_Heritage_Foundation) plaque for this memorial erroneously states that Walter Allward studied under [Emanuel Hahn](:Emanuel_Hahn); in fact, it was the other way around.For two decades after the war, Canadians would gather on February 27 (known in Canada as \"[Paardeberg Day](:Battle_of_Paardeberg)\") around memorials to the South African War to say prayers and honour veterans. This continued until the end of the [First World War](:First_World_War), when [Armistice Day](:Armistice_Day) (later called [Remembrance Day](:Remembrance_Day%29) began to be observed on November 11. The monument was unveiled in 1910 by Sir John French.  "^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
