<http://dbpedia.org/resource/San_Francisco> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "[MSA](:Metropolitan_statistical_area)—$591.9 billion (4th)**San Francisco** (; [Spanish](:Spanish_language) for \"[Saint Francis](:Francis_of_Assisi)\"), officially the **City and County of San Francisco**, is the commercial, financial, and cultural center of [Northern California](:Northern_California). The city proper is the [fourth most populous in California](:List_of_California_cities_by_population) and [17th most populous in the United States](:List_of_United_States_cities_by_population), with 815,201 residents as of 2021. It covers a land area of 46.9 square miles (121 square kilometers), at the end of the [San Francisco Peninsula](:San_Francisco_Peninsula), making it the second most densely populated large U.S. city after [New York City](:New_York_City), and the [fifth most densely populated U.S. county](:County_statistics_of_the_United_States), behind only four of the five [New York City boroughs](:New_York_City_boroughs). Among the 331 U.S. cities proper with more than 100,000 residents, San Francisco was ranked first by per capita income (at $133,856) and fifth by aggregate income as of 2019. Colloquial nicknames for San Francisco include *SF*, *San Fran*, *The City*, *Frisco*, and *Baghdad by the Bay*.San Francisco and the surrounding [San Francisco Bay Area](:San_Francisco_Bay_Area) are a global center of economic activity and the arts and sciences, spurred by leading universities, [high-tech](:IT), healthcare, [FIRE](:FIRE_economy), and professional services sectors. As of 2020, the metropolitan area, with 6.7 million residents, ranked 5th by GDP ($874 billion) and 2nd by GDP per capita ($131,082) across the [OECD](:OECD) countries, ahead of [global cities](:Global_city) like [Paris](:Paris), [London](:London), and [Singapore](:Singapore). San Francisco anchors the [12th most populous metropolitan statistical area](:List_of_metropolitan_statistical_areas) in the United States with 4.7 million residents, and the fourth-largest by economic output, with a GDP of $592 billion in 2019. The wider [San Jose–San Francisco–Oakland, CA Combined Statistical Area](:San_Jose%E2%80%93San_Francisco%E2%80%93Oakland%2C_CA_Combined_Statistical_Area) is the fifth most populous, with 9.6 million residents, and the third-largest by economic output, with a GDP of $0.5 trillion in 2020. Of the 105 [primary statistical areas](:Primary_statistical_area) in the U.S. with over 500,000 residents, this CSA had the highest GDP per capita in 2019, at $112,910. In the same year, San Francisco proper had a GDP of $200.5 billion, and a GDP per capita of $228,118. San Francisco was ranked seventh in the world and third in the United States on the Global Financial Centres Index as of March 2022.As of June 2022, the Bay Area was home to four of the world's fifteen largest companies by market capitalization, and the city proper is headquarters to companies such as [Wells Fargo](:Wells_Fargo), [Salesforce](:Salesforce%2Ecom), [Uber](:Uber), [First Republic Bank](:First_Republic_Bank), [Airbnb](:Airbnb), [Twitter](:Twitter%2C_Inc%2E), [Block](:Square%2C_Inc%2E), [Levi's](:Levi_Strauss_%26_Co%2E), [Gap](:Gap_Inc%2E), [Dropbox](:Dropbox_%28service%29), [PG&E](:Pacific_Gas_and_Electric_Company), [Lyft](:Lyft), and [Cruise](:Cruise_%28autonomous_vehicle%29), although the [COVID-19 pandemic in the United States](:COVID-19_pandemic_in_the_United_States) has accelerated the exodus of business from downtown San Francisco. The city is home to a number of educational and cultural institutions, such as the [University of California, San Francisco](:University_of_California%2C_San_Francisco) (UCSF), the [University of San Francisco](:University_of_San_Francisco) (USF), [San Francisco State University](:San_Francisco_State_University) (SFSU), the [de Young Museum](:De_Young_Museum), the [San Francisco Museum of Modern Art](:San_Francisco_Museum_of_Modern_Art), the [San Francisco Symphony](:San_Francisco_Symphony), the [San Francisco Ballet](:San_Francisco_Ballet), the [San Francisco Opera](:San_Francisco_Opera), the [SFJAZZ Center](:SFJAZZ_Center), the [California Academy of Sciences](:California_Academy_of_Sciences), the [San Francisco Giants](:San_Francisco_Giants), and the [Golden State Warriors](:Golden_State_Warriors). A popular tourist destination, San Francisco is known for its steep rolling [hills](:List_of_hills_in_San_Francisco) and [eclectic mix of architecture](:Architecture_of_San_Francisco) across [varied neighborhoods](:San_Francisco_neighborhoods), as well as its cool summers, [fog](:San_Francisco_fog), and landmarks, including the [Golden Gate Bridge](:Golden_Gate_Bridge), [cable cars](:San_Francisco_cable_car_system), [Alcatraz](:Alcatraz_Federal_Penitentiary), and [Chinatown](:Chinatown%2C_San_Francisco) and [Mission](:Mission_District%2C_San_Francisco) districts.San Francisco was founded on June 29, 1776, when [colonists from Spain](:Spanish_colonization_of_the_Americas) established the [Presidio of San Francisco](:Presidio_of_San_Francisco) at the [Golden Gate](:Golden_Gate) and [Mission San Francisco de Asís](:Mission_San_Francisco_de_As%C3%ADs) a few miles away, both named for [Francis of Assisi](:Francis_of_Assisi). The [California Gold Rush](:California_Gold_Rush) of 1849 brought rapid growth, transforming an unimportant hamlet into a busy port making it the largest city on the [West Coast](:West_Coast_of_the_United_States) at the time; between 1870 and 1900, approximately one quarter of California's population resided in the city proper. In 1856, San Francisco became a [consolidated city-county](:Consolidated_city-county). After three-quarters of the city was destroyed by the [1906 earthquake and fire](:1906_San_Francisco_earthquake), it was quickly rebuilt, hosting the [Panama-Pacific International Exposition](:Panama-Pacific_International_Exposition_%281915%29) nine years later. In [World War II](:World_War_II), it was a major port of embarkation for [naval service](:United_States_Department_of_the_Navy) members shipping out to the [Pacific Theater](:Pacific_Ocean_theater_of_World_War_II). It then became the birthplace of the [United Nations](:United_Nations) in 1945. After the war, the confluence of returning servicemen, significant [immigration](:Immigration_to_the_United_States), [liberalizing](:Modern_liberalism_in_the_United_States) attitudes, the rise of the \"[beatnik](:Beatnik)\" and \"[hippie](:Hippie)\" countercultures, the [sexual revolution](:Sexual_revolution), the [peace movement](:Peace_movement) growing from [opposition to United States involvement in the Vietnam War](:Opposition_to_United_States_involvement_in_the_Vietnam_War), and other factors led to the [Summer of Love](:Summer_of_Love) and the [gay rights](:LGBT_rights_in_the_United_States) movement, cementing San Francisco as a center of [liberal activism in the United States](:Modern_liberalism_in_the_United_States). More recently, statewide [droughts in California](:Droughts_in_California) have strained the city's [water security](:Water_security)."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
