<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Brest,_Belarus> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Brest** (Template:Lang-be, Template:IPA-be; Template:Lang-ru, Template:IPA-ru; Template:Lang-uk; Template:Lang-lt; Template:Lang-pl; Template:Lang-yi, formerly **Brest-Litovsk** (Template:Lang-ru; Template:Lang-be; Template:Lang-lt; Template:Lang-pl, Template:Lang-yi, Brest-on-the-Bug (Template:Lang-pl), is a city (population 350,616 in 2019) in [Belarus](:Belarus) at the border with [Poland](:Poland) opposite the Polish city of [Terespol](:Terespol), where the [Bug](:Bug_%28river%29) and [Mukhavets](:Mukhavets) rivers meet, making it a [border town](:Border_town). It is the capital city of the [Brest Region](:Brest_Region).Brest is a historical site for many cultures, as it hosted important historical events, such as the [Union of Brest](:Union_of_Brest) and [Treaty of Brest-Litovsk](:Treaty_of_Brest-Litovsk). Furthermore, the [Brest Fortress](:Brest_Fortress) was recognized by the [Soviet Union](:Soviet_Union) as a [Hero Fortress](:Hero_Fortress) in honour of the [defense of Brest Fortress](:Defense_of_Brest_Fortress) in June 1941.From [Late Middle Ages](:Late_Middle_Ages) to 1795, the city was part of the [Grand Duchy of Lithuania](:Grand_Duchy_of_Lithuania), which later became a part of the [Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth](:Polish%E2%80%93Lithuanian_Commonwealth) from 1569. In 1795, it was incorporated into the [Russian Empire](:Russian_Empire) with the [Third Partition of Poland](:Third_Partition_of_Poland). After [World War I](:World_War_I), the city became part of by the [Second Polish Republic](:Second_Polish_Republic). In 1939, during the [Invasion of Poland](:Invasion_of_Poland) by Nazi Germany and the Soviet Union, the city was first captured by the [Wehrmacht](:Wehrmacht) and soon passed on to the USSR per the [German–Soviet Frontier Treaty](:German%E2%80%93Soviet_Frontier_Treaty). In 1941, it was retaken by the Germans during [Operation Barbarossa](:Operation_Barbarossa). In 1944, it was taken by the Soviet Red Army during the [Lublin–Brest offensive](:Lublin%E2%80%93Brest_offensive). The city was in the [Belarusian SSR](:Byelorussian_Soviet_Socialist_Republic) until the [breakup of the USSR](:Dissolution_of_the_Soviet_Union) in 1991. Since then, Brest has been part of [Belarus](:Belarus)."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
