<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Monument_on_the_site_of_the_death_of_Despot_Stefan_Lazarević> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The **Monument to Despot Stefan Lazarević** (Template:Lang-sr is in the village of [Crkvine](:Crkvine_%28Mladenovac%29) by [Mladenovac](:Mladenovac), [Serbia](:Serbia), in the [courtyard](:Courtyard) of the [church](:Church_%28building%29) of St. [prophet](:Prophet) [Elijah](:Elijah). It is a [marble](:Marble) [monument](:Monument) with a [medieval](:Middle_Ages) record of the [death](:Death) of [Despot](:Despot_%28court_title%29) [Stefan Lazarević](:Stefan_Lazarevi%C4%87). It was declared a [Monument of Culture of Exceptional Importance](:Monuments_of_Culture_of_Exceptional_Importance_%28Serbia%29) in 1979, and it is protected by the [Republic of Serbia](:Republic_of_Serbia). The monument is a [column](:Column) of glazed white marble of height 186 cm, width 68 cm, and a thickness of 26 cm. The main inscription is written in [calligraphic](:Calligraphy) letters, on the monument's west side, below an engraved three-sided [cross](:Cross). The monument was erected by Lazarević's companion Đurađ Zubrović immediately after Lazarević's death in 1427.A translation of the monument's inscription:*I, Despot Stefan, son of the [Saint Prince Lazar](:Tsar_Lazar) after presenting was a ruler by a God's grace to all Serbs in [Podunavlje](:Podunavlje) and Posavlje and part of [Hungarian](:Kingdom_of_Hungary) land and [Bosnia](:Kingdom_of_Bosnia) but also in [Zeta coast](:Duklja). And in God given power I spent my lifetime as much as the dear God wanted, 38 years. Thus came the order from King of All and God and the sent [angel](:Angel) told me: \"Go!\" and so my soul departed my poor body at the place named [\"Glava\"](:Rudna_Glava), in the year of 6000. and 900. and 30. and 5, indicate 5, sun circle 19 and moon 19, month July 19. day.*Pious Mr. Despot Stefan, good gentleman, too good and beloved and sweet Mr. Despot! Oh, hard it is to anyone who sees him dead in this place.I, Đurađ Zubrović, sinful servant of God, laid this stone.Forgive me, God*.*"^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Russia__HistoricalEvent__11> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://schema.org/Event> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Martqopi_monastery> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The Martqopi monastery of the Deity (Georgian: მარტყოფის ღვთაების მონასტერი, romanized: mart'q'opis ghvtaebis monast'eri) is a Georgian Orthodox monastery near the village of Martqopi, some 25 km east of Tbilisi, the capital of Georgia. The history of the monastery dates back to stylite practices in the 6th century and is linked by historical tradition with St. Anton, one of the Thirteen Assyrian Fathers. Most of the monastery's extant structures are from the 17th to the 19th century. The monastery is inscribed on the list of the Immovable Cultural Monuments of National Significance of Georgia."@en .
