<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Almaty> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Almaty** (; Template:Lang-kk; Template:IPA-kk, formerly known as **Alma-Ata** (Template:Lang-kk), is the [largest city](:List_of_most_populous_cities_in_Kazakhstan) in [Kazakhstan](:Kazakhstan), with a population of about 2 million. It was the capital of Kazakhstan from 1929 to 1936 as an [autonomous republic](:Kazakh_Autonomous_Soviet_Socialist_Republic) as part of the [Soviet Union](:Soviet_Union), then from 1936 to 1991 as a [union republic](:Kazakh_Soviet_Socialist_Republic) and finally from 1991 as an independent state to 1997 when the government relocated the capital to [Akmola](:Astana) (renamed Astana in 1998, Nur-Sultan in 2019, and back to Astana in 2022).Almaty is still the major commercial, financial, and cultural centre of Kazakhstan, as well as its most populous and most cosmopolitan city. The city is located in the mountainous area of southern Kazakhstan near the border with [Kyrgyzstan](:Kyrgyzstan) in the foothills of the [Trans-Ili Alatau](:Trans-Ili_Alatau) at an elevation of 700–900 m (2,300–3,000 feet), where the Large and Small Almatinka rivers run into the plain. The city, just like [Astana](:Astana) and [Shymkent](:Shymkent), does not belong to any [region](:Administrative_divisions_of_Kazakhstan) and is officially a \"city of state importance\".The city has been part of the [UNESCO Creative Cities Network](:Creative_Cities_Network) in the area of music since November 2017. The city was the host for a 1978 international conference on [Primary Health Care](:Primary_healthcare) where the [Alma Ata Declaration](:Alma_Ata_Declaration) was adopted, marking a paradigm shift in global public health."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Russell_Alger_Memorial_Fountain> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Russell Alger Memorial Fountain** is a [Detroit, Michigan](:Detroit) fountain, one of the \"most successful collaborations\" created by the sculptor, [Daniel Chester French](:Daniel_Chester_French) and architect [Henry Bacon](:Henry_Bacon). The bronze statue was cast by the [Gorham Manufacturing Company](:Gorham_Manufacturing_Company). It is located in [Grand Circus Park](:Grand_Circus_Park) and was dedicated on July 27, 1921.The [Beaux-Arts](:Beaux-Arts_architecture) styled bronze monument consists of a \"spirited\" female personification of \"Michigan\" with one hand raised in greeting while the other holds a sword and shield. The crest of the state of Michigan is on the shield in shallow relief. There is a [bas relief](:Bas_relief) portrait of Alger on the granite base. Lion heads on the base serve as founts to pour water into the surrounding bowl.[Russell Alger](:Russell_Alger) (1836–1907) was a [Civil War](:United_States_Civil_War) veteran (he enlisted as a private and ended the war as a general), politician (Governor of Michigan, U.S. Senator from Michigan, and U.S. Secretary of War), and a very successful businessman. Following Alger's death a \"memorial society\" of Civil War veterans convened and commissioned the work."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
