<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Robert_Mills_(architect)> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Robert Mills** (August 12, 1781 – March 3, 1855) was a [South Carolina](:South_Carolina) architect known for designing both the first [Washington Monument](:Washington_Monument_%28Baltimore%29), located in [Baltimore](:Baltimore), [Maryland](:Maryland), as well as the better known [monument](:Washington_Monument) to the first president in the nation's capital, [Washington, DC](:Washington%2C_DC). He is sometimes said to be the first native-born American to be professionally trained as an architect. [Charles Bulfinch](:Charles_Bulfinch) of [Boston](:Boston) perhaps has a clearer claim to this honor.Mills studied in [Charleston, South Carolina](:Charleston%2C_South_Carolina), as a student in the lower school at the [College of Charleston](:College_of_Charleston) and of Irish architect [James Hoban](:James_Hoban), and later worked with him on his commission for the [White House](:White_House). This became the official home of US presidents. Both Hoban and Mills were [Freemasons](:Freemasons). Mills also studied and worked with [Benjamin Henry Latrobe](:Benjamin_Henry_Latrobe) of [Philadelphia](:Philadelphia). He designed numerous buildings in Philadelphia, Baltimore, and South Carolina, where he was appointed as superintendent of public buildings. His Washington Monument in Washington, DC was not completed until 1885, 30 years after his death."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
