<http://dbpedia.org/resource/George_Gordon_Meade_Memorial> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The **George Gordon Meade Memorial**, also known as the **Meade Memorial** or ***Major General George Gordon Meade***, is a public artwork in [Washington, D.C.](:Washington%2C_D%2EC%2E) honoring [George Meade](:George_Meade), a career military officer from [Pennsylvania](:Pennsylvania) who is best known for defeating General [Robert E. Lee](:Robert_E%2E_Lee) at the [Battle of Gettysburg](:Battle_of_Gettysburg). The monument is sited on the 300 block of [Pennsylvania Avenue](:Pennsylvania_Avenue) NW in front of the [E. Barrett Prettyman United States Courthouse](:E%2E_Barrett_Prettyman_United_States_Courthouse). It was originally located at [Union Square](:Union_Square_%28Washington%2C_D%2EC%2E%29), but was removed and placed in storage for fourteen years before being installed at its current location. The statue was sculpted by [Charles Grafly](:Charles_Grafly), an educator and founder of the [National Sculpture Society](:National_Sculpture_Society), and was a gift from the state of Pennsylvania. Prominent attendees at the dedication ceremony in 1927 included President [Calvin Coolidge](:Calvin_Coolidge), Governor [John Stuchell Fisher](:John_Stuchell_Fisher), Secretary of the Treasury [Andrew W. Mellon](:Andrew_W%2E_Mellon), and Senator [Simeon D. Fess](:Simeon_D%2E_Fess).The memorial is one of [eighteen Civil War monuments](:Civil_War_Monuments_in_Washington%2C_D%2EC%2E) in Washington, D.C., which were collectively listed on the [National Register of Historic Places](:National_Register_of_Historic_Places) in 1978. The marble and granite sculpture, which includes depictions of Meade and seven allegorical figures, rests on a granite base and granite platform. It is surrounded by a public plaza and small park. The monument is owned and maintained by the [National Park Service](:National_Park_Service), a federal agency of the [Interior Department](:United_States_Department_of_the_Interior).Another monument to Meade by sculptor [Henry Kirke Bush-Brown](:Henry_Kirke_Bush-Brown) is on the [Gettysburg Battlefield](:Gettysburg_Battlefield). It was dedicated in 1896 as the first equestrian monument erected on the battlefield and is one of scores of [Gettysburg monuments and markers](:List_of_monuments_of_the_Gettysburg_Battlefield%23United_States_monuments)."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
