<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Milwaukee> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Milwaukee** ( [*mil-WAW-kee*](:Help%3APronunciation_respelling_key%29), officially the **City of Milwaukee**, is both the most populous and most densely populated city in the [U.S. state](:U%2ES%2E_state) of [Wisconsin](:Wisconsin) and the [county seat](:County_seat) of [Milwaukee County](:Milwaukee_County%2C_Wisconsin). With a population of 577,222 at the [2020 census](:2020_United_States_census), Milwaukee is the [31st largest city in the United States](:List_of_United_States_cities_by_population), the fifth-largest city in the [Midwestern United States](:Midwestern_United_States), and the second largest city on [Lake Michigan](:Lake_Michigan)'s shore behind [Chicago](:Chicago).It is the main cultural and economic center of the [Milwaukee metropolitan area](:Milwaukee_metropolitan_area), the fourth-most densely populated [metropolitan area](:Metropolitan_area) in the [Midwest](:Midwestern_United_States). Milwaukee is considered a [global city](:Global_city), categorized as \"Gamma minus\" by the [Globalization and World Cities Research Network](:Globalization_and_World_Cities_Research_Network), with a regional [GDP](:List_of_U%2ES%2E_metropolitan_areas_by_GDP) of over $102 billion in 2020.Today, Milwaukee is one of the most [ethnically](:Ethnicity) and [culturally diverse](:Cultural_diversity) cities in the U.S. However, it continues to be one of the most racially segregated, largely as a result of early-20th-century [redlining](:Redlining). Its [history](:History_of_Milwaukee) was heavily influenced by German immigrants in the 19th century, and it continues to be a center for [German-American](:German-American) culture, specifically becoming well known for its [brewing industry](:Beer_in_Milwaukee). In recent years, Milwaukee has been undergoing its largest construction boom since [the 1960s](:History_of_Milwaukee%231960_to_1995). Major additions to the city since the turn of the 21st century include the [Wisconsin Center](:Wisconsin_Center), [American Family Field](:American_Family_Field), [The Hop (streetcar system)](:The_Hop_%28streetcar%29), an expansion to the [Milwaukee Art Museum](:Milwaukee_Art_Museum), [Milwaukee Repertory Theater](:Milwaukee_Repertory_Theater), the [Bradley Symphony Center](:Milwaukee_Symphony_Orchestra), and [Discovery World](:Discovery_World), as well as major renovations to the [UW–Milwaukee Panther Arena](:UW%E2%80%93Milwaukee_Panther_Arena). [Fiserv Forum](:Fiserv_Forum) opened in late 2018, and hosts sporting events and concerts. Since 1968, Milwaukee has been home to [Summerfest](:Summerfest), one of the largest music festivals in the world. With regard to education, Milwaukee is home to the [Medical College of Wisconsin](:Medical_College_of_Wisconsin), [UW-Milwaukee](:UW-Milwaukee), [Marquette University](:Marquette_University), [MSOE](:Milwaukee_School_of_Engineering), and several other universities and colleges. The city is home to two [major professional sports teams](:Major_professional_sports_leagues_in_the_United_States_and_Canada) − the [Bucks](:Milwaukee_Bucks) and the [Brewers](:Milwaukee_Brewers). It is home to several [*Fortune* 500](:Fortune_500) companies, including [Northwestern Mutual](:Northwestern_Mutual), [WEC Energy Group](:WEC_Energy_Group), [Rockwell Automation](:Rockwell_Automation), and [Harley-Davidson](:Harley-Davidson)."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
