<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Glenfinnan_Monument> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The **Glenfinnan Monument** is a [Category A listed](:Listed_building%23Scotland) monument in [Glenfinnan](:Glenfinnan), Scotland, erected in 1814 and dedicated to the soldiers of [Loudon's Highlanders](:Loudon%27s_Highlanders), who fought in the [Jacobite rising of 1745](:Jacobite_rising_of_1745).By 1814, the [Jacobite](:Jacobitism) cause was no longer a political threat to the [Hanoverian monarchy](:Hanoverian_monarchy). [Alexander Macdonald of Glenaladale](:Clan_Macdonald_of_Clanranald), a minor branch of the [Clan Donald](:Clan_Donald), built the tower to commemorate the raising of the standard of the [Young Pretender](:Young_Pretender). The tower, which is 59 ft (18 m) in height, was designed by Scottish architect [James Gillespie Graham](:James_Gillespie_Graham). The statue of an unknown Highlander, referred to at the point of commission as [Charles Edward Stewart](:Charles_Edward_Stewart), by [John Greenshields](:John_Greenshields), was added in 1835.The monument's location at Glenfinnan was made possible because of a new road (now the [A830](:A830_road%29), built by [Thomas Telford](:Thomas_Telford) and opened in 1812, between [Fort William](:Fort_William%2C_Highland) and [Arisaig](:Arisaig).Since 1938, the monument has been in the care of the [National Trust for Scotland](:National_Trust_for_Scotland). The Trust has constructed a visitor centre, providing tickets, information, exhibitions, a shop, a café and toilets. The tower is also a monument to Alexander Macdonald, who died before its completion. Jacobite enthusiasts gather at the tower each year on 19 August to remember the rising of 1745."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
