<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Surabaya> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Surabaya** (Template:Lang-jv or Template:Lang-jv; Template:IPA-jv; Template:IPA-id is the [capital city](:Capital_city) of the [Indonesian province](:Provinces_of_Indonesia) of [East Java](:East_Java) and the [second-largest city](:List_of_Indonesian_cities_by_population) in Indonesia, after [Jakarta](:Jakarta). Located on the northeastern border of [Java](:Java) island, on the [Madura Strait](:Madura_Strait), it is one of the earliest port cities in Southeast Asia. According to the [National Development Planning Agency](:Government_of_Indonesia), Surabaya is one of the [four main central cities of Indonesia](:Regions_of_Indonesia%23Development_regions), alongside [Jakarta](:Jakarta), [Medan](:Medan), and [Makassar](:Makassar). The city has a population of 2.87 million within its [city limits](:City_limits) at the 2020 census and 9.5 million in the extended [Surabaya metropolitan area](:Surabaya_metropolitan_area), making it the [second-largest metropolitan area](:List_of_metropolitan_areas_in_Indonesia) in Indonesia.The city was settled in the 10th century by the [Kingdom of Janggala](:Janggala), one of the two [Javanese](:Java) kingdoms that was formed in 1045 when [Airlangga](:Airlangga) abdicated his throne in favor of his two sons. In the late 15th and 16th centuries, Surabaya grew to be a [duchy](:Duchy_of_Surabaya), a major political and military power as well as a port in eastern Java, probably under the [Majapahit](:Majapahit) empire.At that time, Surabaya was already a major trading port, owing to its location on the River Brantas delta and the trade route between [Malacca](:Malacca) and the [Spice Islands](:Spice_Islands) via the [Java Sea](:Java_Sea). During the decline of Majapahit, the lord of Surabaya resisted the rise of the [Demak Sultanate](:Demak_Sultanate) and only submitted to its rule in 1530. Surabaya became independent after the death of Sultan Trenggana of Demak in 1546.From the 18th century until the mid-20th century, Surabaya was the largest city in the [Dutch East Indies](:Dutch_East_Indies), and the center of trading in the Indonesian archipelago, which was then a competitor to [Shanghai](:Shanghai) and [Hong Kong](:Hong_Kong).The city is known as *Kota Pahlawan* (the city of heroes) due to the importance of the [Battle of Surabaya](:Battle_of_Surabaya) during the [Indonesian National Revolution](:Indonesian_National_Revolution). The city is one of the important financial, commercial, industrial, transportation, and entertainment hubs of the archipelago, arguably second only to Jakarta, and the [Port of Tanjung Perak](:Port_of_Tanjung_Perak) is Indonesia's second-busiest seaport located on northern Surabaya. The city is also known for being one of the cleanest and greenest in Indonesia.Surabaya has been one of the most important and busiest trading city ports in Asia. Principal exports from the port include [sugar](:Sugar), [tobacco](:Tobacco), and [coffee](:Coffee). Its rich history as a trading port has led to a strong financial infrastructure with modern economic institutions such as banks, insurance, and sound export-import companies. The economy is influenced by the recent growth in international industries and the completion of the [Suramadu Bridge](:Suramadu_Bridge). The high potential and economic activities make the city an attractive destination to foreign investors.The city is home to a large [shipyard](:Shipyard) and numerous specialized naval schools. The [Bank of Indonesia](:Bank_of_Indonesia) has also made plans for Surabaya to be the Islamic financial center of Indonesia."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
