<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Hôtel_de_Blossac> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The ***Hôtel de Blossac*** is an 18th-century **[hôtel particulier](:H%C3%B4tel_particulier)** in the historic center of [Rennes](:Rennes), [Brittany](:Brittany_%28administrative_region%29).The building, which has two main wings, was constructed in 1728. (A fire in 1720 had destroyed much of the city of Rennes.) The architect is said to have been [Jacques Gabriel](:Jacques_Gabriel). The building has a unique [classical architecture](:Classicism) for [Brittany](:Brittany), including its size, the assembly of several architectural components, and its grand staircase.Property of the La Bourdonnaye family, earls of Blossac, for nearly two centuries, it was leased by the city as the residence of the commander in chief of the province of Brittany, with a pomp reminiscent of the court of France. At the French Revolution, it was divided into apartments and became a residential building, where the author [Paul Féval](:Paul_F%C3%A9val%2C_p%C3%A8re) was born.The building was designated as a historic monument in 1947, and underwent a complete exterior restoration spread over three decades, while the apartments give way to offices. It has been wholly owned by the state since 1982 and houses, in refurbished and restored rooms, the **[Direction régionale des affaires culturelles](:Direction_r%C3%A9gionale_des_affaires_culturelles)** (DRAC, Regional Directorate of Cultural Affairs of Brittany) and in service quarters, the **service territorial de l’architecture et du patrimoine** (Territorial Service of architecture and heritage of [Ille-et-Vilaine](:Ille-et-Vilaine%29)."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
