<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Warsaw> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Warsaw** (Template:Lang-pl,  ), officially the **Capital City of Warsaw**, is the capital and [largest city](:List_of_cities_and_towns_in_Poland) of [Poland](:Poland). The metropolis stands on the [River Vistula](:Vistula) in east-central Poland, and its population is officially estimated at 1.86 million residents within a [greater metropolitan area](:Warsaw_metropolitan_area) of 3.1 million residents, which makes Warsaw the [6th most-populous city](:List_of_cities_in_the_European_Union_by_population_within_city_limits) in the [European Union](:European_Union). The city area measures 517 km2 (200 sq mi) and comprises [18 districts](:Districts_and_neighbourhoods_of_Warsaw), while the metropolitan area covers 6,100 km2 (2,355 sq mi). Warsaw is an Alpha [global city](:Global_city), a major cultural, political and [economic hub](:Financial_centre), and the country's seat of government.Warsaw traces its origins to a small [fishing town](:Fishing_village) in [Masovia](:Masovia). The city rose to prominence in the late 16th century, when [Sigismund III](:Sigismund_III) decided to move the Polish capital and his royal court from [Kraków](:Krak%C3%B3w). Warsaw served as the de facto capital of the [Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth](:Polish%E2%80%93Lithuanian_Commonwealth) until 1795, and subsequently as the seat of [Napoleon](:Napoleon)'s [Duchy of Warsaw](:Duchy_of_Warsaw). The 19th century and its [Industrial Revolution](:Industrial_Revolution) brought a demographic boom which made it one of the largest and most densely-populated cities in Europe. Known then for its elegant architecture and boulevards, Warsaw was [bombed](:Invasion_of_Poland) and [besieged](:Siege_of_Warsaw_%281939%29) at the start of [World War II](:World_War_II) in 1939. Much of the historic city was destroyed and its diverse population decimated by the [Ghetto Uprising](:Warsaw_Ghetto_Uprising) in 1943, the general [Warsaw Uprising](:Warsaw_Uprising) in 1944 and [systematic razing](:Destruction_of_Warsaw).Warsaw is served by two international airports, the busiest being [Warsaw Chopin](:Warsaw_Chopin_Airport) and the smaller [Warsaw Modlin](:Warsaw_Modlin_Airport) intended for low-cost carriers. Major public transport services operating in the city include the [Warsaw Metro](:Warsaw_Metro), [buses](:Bus_transport_in_Warsaw), [commuter rail service](:Szybka_Kolej_Miejska_%28Warsaw%29) and an extensive [tram network](:Trams_in_Warsaw). The city is a significant centre of research and development, [business process outsourcing](:Business_process_outsourcing), and [information technology outsourcing](:Information_technology_outsourcing). The [Warsaw Stock Exchange](:Warsaw_Stock_Exchange) is the largest and most important in [Central](:Central_Europe) and [Eastern Europe](:Eastern_Europe). [Frontex](:Frontex), the [European Union agency](:Agencies_of_the_European_Union) for external border security, and [ODIHR](:ODIHR), one of the principal institutions of the [Organization for Security and Co-operation in Europe](:Organization_for_Security_and_Co-operation_in_Europe), have their headquarters in Warsaw. Jointly with Frankfurt and Paris, Warsaw features one of the highest number of skyscrapers in the European Union, while [Varso Place](:Varso) is the tallest building in the EU as of 2022.The city is home to renowned universities such as the [University of Warsaw](:University_of_Warsaw), the [Warsaw University of Technology](:Warsaw_University_of_Technology), [SGH Warsaw School of Economics](:SGH_Warsaw_School_of_Economics), [Chopin University of Music](:Chopin_University_of_Music) and [Kozminski University](:Kozminski_University). It also hosts the [Polish Academy of Sciences](:Polish_Academy_of_Sciences), [National Philharmonic Orchestra](:Warsaw_National_Philharmonic_Orchestra), the [National Museum](:National_Museum%2C_Warsaw), [Zachęta](:Zach%C4%99ta) Art Gallery and the [Warsaw Grand Theatre](:Grand_Theatre%2C_Warsaw), the largest of its kind in the world. The reconstructed Old Town, which represents examples of nearly every European architectural style and [historical period](:List_of_time_periods), was listed as a [World Heritage Site](:World_Heritage_Site) in 1980. Other architectural attractions include the [Royal Castle](:Royal_Castle%2C_Warsaw), [Sigismund's Column](:Sigismund%27s_Column), the [Wilanów Palace](:Wilan%C3%B3w_Palace), the [Palace on the Isle](:Palace_on_the_Isle), [St. John's Archcathedral](:St%2E_John%27s_Archcathedral%2C_Warsaw), [Main Market Square](:Old_Town_Market_Place%2C_Warsaw), and numerous churches and mansions along the [Royal Route](:Royal_Route%2C_Warsaw). The [Warsaw Zoo](:Warsaw_Zoo) is among the largest and most-visited zoological gardens in the country. The city possesses thriving arts and club scenes, gourmet restaurants and large [urban green spaces](:Urban_green_space), with around a quarter of the city's area occupied by parks. In sports, the city is known as the home of the top-tier football club [Legia Warsaw](:Legia_Warsaw), the [Warsaw Marathon](:Warsaw_Marathon) and Poland's national football stadium [Stadion Narodowy](:Stadion_Narodowy)."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
