<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Lahore> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Lahore** ( [*lə-HOR*](:Help%3APronunciation_respelling_key); Template:Lang-pnb Template:IPA-pa; Template:Lang-ur Template:IPA-ur is the second [most populous city](:List_of_cities_in_Pakistan_by_population) in [Pakistan](:Pakistan) after [Karachi](:Karachi) and 26th [most populous city in the world](:List_of_largest_cities), with a population of over 13 million. It is the capital of the [province](:Provinces_of_Pakistan) of [Punjab](:Punjab%2C_Pakistan) where it is the largest city. Lahore is one of Pakistan's major industrial and economic hubs, with an estimated GDP ([PPP](:Purchasing_power_parity%29) of $84 billion as of 2019. It is the largest city as well as historic and cultural capital of the wider [Punjab region](:Punjab_region), and is one of Pakistan's most [socially liberal](:Social_liberalism), [progressive](:Progressivism), and [cosmopolitan](:Cosmopolitanism) cities. It is situated in [north-east of the country](:Geography_of_Pakistan), close to the [International border](:India-Pakistan_border) with [India](:India).Lahore's origins reach into antiquity. The city has been inhabited for at least two millenia, although it rose to prominence in the 10th century. Lahore was the capital of multiple empires throughout the course of its history, including the [Hindu Shahis](:Hindu_Shahi), [Ghaznavids](:Ghaznavids), and [Delhi Sultanate](:Delhi_Sultanate) in the medieval era. Lahore reached the height of its splendour under the [Mughal Empire](:Mughal_Empire) between the late 16th and early 18th century and served as its capital city for many years. [The city was captured](:Nader_Shah%27s_invasion_of_India) by the forces of the [Afsharid](:Afsharid_dynasty) ruler [Nader Shah](:Nader_Shah) in 1739. Although the Mughal authority was re-established, it fell into a period of decay while being contested between the Afghans and the Sikhs between 1748 and 1798. Lahore eventually became the capital of the [Sikh Empire](:Sikh_Empire) in the early 19th century, regaining some of its lost grandeur. Lahore was annexed to the [British Raj](:British_Raj) in 1849, and became capital of [British Punjab](:Punjab_Province_%28British_India%29). Lahore was central to the independence movements of both India and Pakistan, with the city being the site of both the [declaration of Indian Independence](:Purna_Swaraj), and the [resolution calling for the establishment of Pakistan](:Lahore_Resolution). It experienced some of the worst rioting during the [Partition](:Partition_of_British_India) period preceding Pakistan's independence. Following the success of the [Pakistan Movement](:Pakistan_Movement) and subsequent partition of British India in 1947, Lahore was declared the capital of Pakistan's Punjab province.Lahore exerts a strong cultural influence over Pakistan. It is a UNESCO [City of Literature](:City_of_Literature) and major center for Pakistan's publishing industry; Lahore remains the foremost center of Pakistan's literary scene. The city is also a major centre of education in [Pakistan](:Pakistan), with some of Pakistan's leading universities based in the city. For many years, Lahore was home to Pakistan's film industry, [Lollywood](:Lollywood), though in recent years most filming has shifted to [Karachi](:Karachi). Lahore is a major centre of [*Qawwali* music](:Qawwali). The city also hosts much of [Pakistan's tourist industry](:Tourism_in_Pakistan), with major attractions including the [Walled City](:Walled_City_of_Lahore), the famous [Badshahi](:Badshahi_Mosque) and [Wazir Khan](:Wazir_Khan_Mosque) mosques, as well as several [Sikh](:Sikh) and [Sufi](:Sufi) shrines. Lahore is also home to the [Lahore Fort](:Lahore_Fort) and [Shalimar Gardens](:Shalimar_Gardens%2C_Lahore), both of which are [UNESCO](:UNESCO) [World Heritage Sites](:World_Heritage_Site)."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
