<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Newkirk_Viaduct_Monument> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "The **Newkirk Viaduct Monument** (also, **Newkirk Monument**) is a 15-foot white marble obelisk in the [West Philadelphia](:West_Philadelphia) neighborhood of [Philadelphia, Pennsylvania](:Philadelphia%2C_Pennsylvania). Installed in 1839, it is inscribed with the names of 51 railroad builders and executives, among other information.Designed by [Thomas Ustick Walter](:Thomas_Ustick_Walter), a future [Architect of the Capitol](:Architect_of_the_Capitol), the monument was erected by the [Philadelphia, Wilmington and Baltimore Railroad](:Philadelphia%2C_Wilmington_and_Baltimore_Railroad) to mark its completion of [a bridge](:Gray%27s_Ferry_Bridge) across the [Schuylkill River](:Schuylkill_River) and the first railroad line south from Philadelphia. The monument, which originally sat about 700 feet from the riverbank, was moved sometime after 1927 about 600 feet further inland, where it sat for decades by the main line that became [Amtrak's](:Amtrak) [Northeast Corridor](:Northeast_Corridor). In 2016, it was moved to its present location, about 100 feet from the river's edge at the north end of the Bartram's Mile section of the [Schuylkill River Trail](:Schuylkill_River_Trail)."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
